comments[31]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-27T14:00:55.774Z","harness:codex","Progress tranche: added public SDK output-budget contracts for every built-in command, deterministic degradation ladders, output classes, override resolution, token estimation, runtime discovery policy, and a public surface snapshot. The 95-surface and 17-command ratchets now enforce declared ceilings. This does not yet satisfy the full item: runtime enforcement across every command/format, disclosed dropped-field envelopes, and universal override/no-budget flags remain. Returning the item to open so only actively edited work is in progress."
  "2026-07-27T14:07:53.283Z","harness:codex","Delivery PR: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/774 at implementation head 8173f7f73. This PR ships the public SDK declaration and enforcement-baseline tranche; the item remains open for universal runtime degradation and disclosure."
  "2026-07-28T11:53:12.779Z","harness:codex","Progress tranche evidence: this PR adds exact rendered accounting for dependency tree/graph/context, bounded and disclosed activity defaults with an explicit unbounded opt-out, and a failure-capable 23-surface contract-owned PR gate with representative scale and negative control. Full runtime enforcement across every command/format and the universal override/no-budget escape hatch remain, so this broader feature returns to open."
  "2026-07-28T11:57:09.226Z","harness:codex","Delivery PR: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/795 at implementation head 862e88f71. The PR description links canonical PM records and records full coverage, isolated consumer, security, compatibility, and token-gate evidence."
  "2026-07-30T11:24:18.690Z","harness:codex","Tranche evidence: built-in intent ceilings now emit budget/estimate/verdict/degradation receipts and graph/health defaults are bounded; exact token and coverage gates pass. This parent remains open for universal declarative budgets across all diagnostic/read surfaces."
  "2026-07-30T11:30:59.619Z","harness:codex","PR evidence: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/829 reviews cohesive SDK context budgets, universal row contracts, bounded graph/health diagnostics, quality ratchets, and exact-head commit e53544cd1."
  "2026-07-30T11:54:26.185Z","harness:codex","Evidence clarification: e53544cd1 identifies the implementation commit. Exact-head review and merge proof is maintained on PR #829 because every append-only PM evidence mutation necessarily creates a newer Git head."
  "2026-07-30T13:06:09.077Z","harness:codex","Review-fix evidence for PR #829: TDD reproduced the truthful-budget regression where an explicit 1-token request could return an over-budget receipt. The SDK now rejects explicit budgets below 256 with structured invalid_argument_value metadata, preserves declaration fallback only for absent/malformed inputs, and uses command-agnostic recovery text. Focused SDK tests pass and the full sandboxed suite passed 6,578 tests across 406 files at exact 100/100/100/100 coverage. Strict lint, typecheck, static-quality, token-budget, SDK surface, contract, and docs/skills gates pass."
  "2026-07-30T13:39:02.911Z","harness:codex","Exact-head Greptile TDD evidence: a focused regression reproduced self-inconsistent intent accounting (56 reported tokens versus 57 for the serialized envelope). Receipt measurement now converges against the object that contains it, absent built-in source metadata falls back to core instead of disappearing from JSON, and contract descriptions/docs explicitly name standard-depth get, two-row list triage, and fifteen-row search discovery defaults plus their override/completeness semantics. The full sandboxed suite passes 6,579 tests across 406 files at exact 100/100/100/100 coverage; lint, typecheck, contracts, and docs gates pass."
  "2026-07-30T13:58:34.981Z","harness:codex","Final CodeRabbit test-hygiene evidence: replaced shared PM_CONTEXT_INTENT_CONTRACTS mutation with a locally scoped declaration passed to resolveContextIntentContract. The resolver now normalizes a missing source to core before returning, so attachContextIntentReceipt consumes a proven invariant without mutating global state. Focused tests and the full 6,579-test suite pass at exact 100/100/100/100 coverage; public SDK signature remains unchanged."
  "2026-07-30T17:15:08.042Z","harness:claude-code","Bounding-surface census 2026-07-30 (evening) at HEAD, recorded here because this item owns the ceiling contract and the census is the shape the contract has to govern.\n\nSeventeen distinct output-bounding flag spellings exist across twenty read commands, and no two commands expose the same set: --after, --brief, --check-only, --depth, --fields, --for, --format, --full, --limit, --max-items, --no-truncate, --sample-limit, --section, --stream, --summary, --token-budget, --blocked-limit. Per command: the four list variants nine each, context eight, search six, get five, next five, deps four, graph four, health four, history three, activity three, events two, contracts two, comments one, notes one, validate one, and files, stats and aggregate zero.\n\nThree findings for the contract.\n\nThe budget flag is on three of twenty and it is on the wrong three. next, context and deps declare --token-budget. list, get and search declare intent budgets, enforce them at egress and reject the flag outright, so a caller who hits a ceiling on those three has no override. This is reachable today: pm list --limit 200 --for triage and pm get <id> --depth deep --for inspect both return a receipt with zero rows and no way to raise the limit.\n\nThe declared ceilings themselves are not reachable on this corpus for three of five intents, measured floors against declared budgets: next execute 1,438 against 1200, context orient 8,417 against 2400, context handoff the same. All five fit on a two-item workspace. A ceiling contract therefore needs a reachability clause, not only an enforcement clause.\n\nBounded is not the same as cheap. Walking all 315 open items through the triage intent costs 158 pages at 6,250 bytes each, roughly 987,500 bytes, against 71,709 bytes for the single unprojected call, because eight chain-invariant metadata blocks are re-emitted on a two-row page. Per row that is 3,125 bytes against 228. The ceiling contract should state the cost of a full walk, not only the cost of a page, or it will keep certifying payloads that are individually small and collectively 13.8 times larger than the form they replaced.\n\nThe two issues that carry the fixes are filed under this item, and the receipt accounting question this item already tracked is unchanged: the receipts now report 5.4 to 5.6 emitted bytes per estimated token, so the estimator remains optimistic against real serialised output."
  "2026-08-01T09:52:25.936Z","harness:claude-code","Coverage measured 2026-08-01 at HEAD on 2026.8.1: 55 of 76 canonical commands in pm contracts --summary declare default_max_estimated_tokens; 21 declare none. The same census found 32 of 76 carrying a placeholder intent, filed as pm-7i97c3 -- both gaps live on the same payload, are found by the same join against the command catalog, and should be closed by the same conformance check rather than by two separate passes. Measured payloads for the budget question itself: pm contracts --summary 9,701 bytes (~2,425 tokens), pm next 9,912 bytes (~2,478 tokens), pm context 8,882 bytes (~2,220 tokens), pm list-open --limit 20 4,837 bytes, pm search --limit 10 4,675 bytes. Documented two-call cold start is therefore about 4,903 tokens against a full per-command help surface of 54,718."
  "2026-08-03T11:15:44.666Z","harness:codex","Live contract delta 2026-08-03: 21 of 76 canonical commands still declare no default_max_estimated_tokens. The missing set includes package and extension utilities, completion helpers, governance extensions, guide, normalize, reindex, search-advanced, templates, test-runs, todos, twin, and vcs. Per-call ceilings also do not solve the cross-call orientation budget owned by pm-hid9g1."
  "2026-08-04T19:54:38.498Z","harness:codex","Implementation tranche evidence 2026-08-04: output budget declarations now carry format-specific TOON and JSON ceilings, classify package-contributed commands, and generate conservative non-null fallbacks. Full runtime contracts pair envelope and budget metadata for all 117 active commands. Static token-budget and context-calibration gates passed; full direct coverage is 100/100/100/100. Packed consumer proof observed create TOON=2000 and JSON=3000. Universal runtime enforcement, omission receipts, resumable degradation, and explicit unbounded opt-in remain open. The nested pm test coverage failure is canonical pm-ay3l0p context leakage, not a direct coverage regression."
  "2026-08-04T20:56:34.442Z","harness:codex","Review evidence: output-budget resolution now preserves normalized full subcommand paths while reusing root-command ceilings, preventing duplicate command keys in the generated contract inventory. Added subcommand and invalid-JSON regressions and regenerated the token-surface baseline from the current build."
  "2026-08-07T11:23:41.448Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence measured 2026-08-07. `pm deps <id>` carries an undeclared default ceiling of 16,000 estimated tokens — the largest default in the surface, roughly three times the whole documented agent cold start — enforced by a private vocabulary (--token-budget, --node-limit, --edge-limit, --max-depth) rather than the universal read controls. On this workspace it truncates 1,341 of 1,381 nodes and 6,645 of 6,694 edges to stay under it, and the number appears in no help text, no contract summary, and no doc. Separately `pm contracts --full --json` emits 389,951 estimated tokens with no default ceiling at all. Both are the same gap this item closes: a ceiling that exists but is not contracted is indistinguishable from no ceiling to the agent that has to plan around it."
  "2026-08-13T08:50:42.666Z","harness:claude-code","Measured 2026-08-13 on the live tracker: the ceiling this item asks for exists and is opt-in, so the default path is unbounded.\n\npm list-open --limit 1 --full  -> 365,315 bytes (~91,300 tokens)\npm list-open --limit 2 --full  -> 378,848 bytes\npm list-open --limit 5 --full  -> 619,662 bytes\npm list-open --limit 10 --full -> 922,471 bytes\npm list-open --limit 20 --full -> 994,450 bytes (~248,600 tokens)\n\nOne row can exceed 90,000 tokens because --full inlines unbounded child collections verbatim. Field sizes for the largest single row: notes 318,223 bytes, comments 36,617, dependencies 2,661, learnings 717. The row's own scalar fields total under 1,200 bytes, so 99.7 percent of the payload is collections whose size no flag on the invocation bounds.\n\nThe bound already works when asked for: pm list-open --limit 20 --full --token-budget 2000 returns 792 bytes. So the degradation machinery this item specifies is built and correct; what is missing is that no default ceiling applies it. A documented projection flag can therefore emit a payload two to three times larger than a frontier agent's entire context window, and nothing in the invocation, the help text or the response warns before it happens.\n\nTwo further observations for the design. First, the per-row cost is not monotone in --limit: limit 1 costs 365 KB and limit 20 costs 994 KB, so cost tracks which items are in the window rather than how many, which means a per-row estimate cannot be derived from the limit and the ceiling has to be enforced during assembly. Second, --brief and the default projection on list-open are byte-identical (both report projection mode 'brief'), so the flag documented as the agent-optimized token saver saves nothing on the most common read; the saving is real only against --compact and --full."
  "2026-08-13T14:31:04.764Z","harness:claude-code","Present-tense conformance table for this feature's premise, measured 2026-08-13 on 2,467 items with the shipped build, read-only default invocations, four bytes per estimated token.\n\nDeclared ceiling versus observed default output, ratio in the last column:\ncomments-audit 4,000 / 416,433 / 104.1x\nnotes (pm-4k6b) 2,000 / 62,596 / 31.3x\nassurance run (graph-composition) 4,000 / 125,517 / 31.4x\nlist 4,000 / 70,771 / 17.7x\nhealth 6,000 / 30,552 / 5.1x\ncomments (pm-doxj) 2,000 / 9,226 / 4.6x\ndeps (pm-doxj) 4,000 / 15,745 / 3.9x\nevents 4,000 / 9,199 / 2.3x\nhistory (pm-4k6b) 4,000 / 5,767 / 1.4x\nsearch 4,000 / 4,414 / 1.1x\nlist-closed no declared ceiling / 3,157,182\n\nSurfaces comfortably inside their ceiling for contrast: context 2,376, next 2,372, contracts --summary 2,537, get 2,386, validate 5,915, stats 137, telemetry 122, templates 33.\n\nTwo facts that shape the implementation rather than just motivating it. First, the degradation path already exists and already works: the same assurance run under --output-budget 500 returns 601 bytes, so what is missing is the default binding, not the mechanism. Second, every overrunning surface is priced by per-item collection depth rather than by item count — pm-doxj carries 138 notes and pm-4k6b carries 70 — so a ceiling enforced by row count alone will not bind them.\n\nFiled pm-s2h0mq for the enforcement gap and the fixture-adequacy defect behind it."
  "2026-08-13T17:12:48.853Z","harness:codex","Evidence for broad default-output-contract tranche: implemented format-aware default ceilings across all 22 SDK read surfaces, deterministic projection/truncation receipts with budget_source=default, explicit --output-budget unbounded escape hatch, SDK JSON/CLI renderer parity, and generated exact-path contract budgets. Exact coverage passed 7,262 tests at 100% statements/branches/functions/lines; docstring coverage, typecheck, ESLint, jscpd, security scan, and pnpm audit passed. Packed npm/Bun acceptance passed, including npx and bunx. This item remains open because mutation/stream surfaces and the complete every-command degradation contract still remain beyond this read-surface tranche."
  "2026-08-13T17:32:18.362Z","harness:codex","Final security note: tracked-file credential scan passed. pnpm audit had already returned zero vulnerabilities during this tranche; two final refresh attempts reached npm advisory API timeouts after retries, so the last live re-query is an external-network limitation rather than a vulnerability result. GitHub Dependabot, code-scanning, and secret-scanning inventories were empty at orientation."
  "2026-08-13T17:40:51.692Z","harness:codex","PR #999 CI fix evidence: docs/skills guide validation was an exhaustive contract consumer and default bounding made its availability list incomplete. It now explicitly requests --output-budget unbounded. Focused docs-skills tests passed 22/22 and the real pnpm quality:docs-skills gate passed."
  "2026-08-13T17:56:24.570Z","harness:codex","PR #999 smoke fix evidence: CI intentionally installs search-advanced before the token-surface gate, while the previous baseline had been generated against a different ambient package set. Reproduced the exact CI sequence, retained the declared 10% headroom, and regenerated rather than weakening implementation budgets: summary_json 24,205-byte ceiling over 22,004 measured; contracts.json 24,066 over 21,878. The bounded --full default now measures 496 bytes (546 ceiling). Full quality:context-eval passes."
  "2026-08-13T18:07:50.221Z","harness:codex","PR #999 review pass started: validating all seven CodeRabbit findings against the exact current head. Active work is limited to pm-5t33or, pm-s2h0mq, pm-pmrae8, and pm-7i97c3; all other items remain released."
  "2026-08-13T18:50:47.876Z","harness:codex","Integrated final verification after all review fixes: 490 files and 7,266 tests passed. Exact coverage is Statements 52,740/52,740, Branches 38,774/38,774, Functions 10,839/10,839, Lines 50,945/50,945 (100/100/100/100). Lint, zero-duplicate detection, static/docstring quality, SDK/package parity, token/context gates, graph/record assurance, release-contract tests, and secret scanning passed. The ESLint suppression ceiling was reduced from 114 to 113 rather than carrying a stale waiver."
  "2026-08-13T23:17:38.428Z","harness:codex","Verification evidence for this PR tranche: recursive budget compaction now reports exact nested paths and truthful pre-omission estimates; token-budget conformance compares contract receipt estimates while independently retaining rendered byte/token ratchets. The linked 32-surface token gate and 24 unit tests pass; unbounded negative control is 10,465 tokens and infeasible intent receipt is verified. Full suite passed 7,280 tests at exact 100/100/100/100. Typecheck, ESLint, zero-clone jscpd, 100% docstring/static quality, package boundary/parity, context calibration, graph composition, record integrity, tracked-secret scan, pnpm audit, changelog check, contract snapshot, packed artifact, npx and bunx all pass. The parent remains open because non-read mutation/stream every-command enforcement is outside this tranche."
  "2026-08-13T23:21:04.288Z","harness:codex","Delivery PR: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/1005 at implementation commit a4b33ccb3. This is the read-output budget and conformance tranche; the broader every-command parent remains open."
  "2026-08-13T23:30:20.510Z","harness:codex","CI evidence: PR #1005 hosted shard aggregation exposed one uncovered fail-closed branch at scripts/release/token-budget-gate.mjs:709 after the complexity refactor. Added an explicit missing-intent-receipt negative control to the already linked gate suite; focused token-budget suite passes 24/24 and ESLint is clean. This preserves rather than weakens the exact 100% ratchet."
  "2026-08-13T23:35:52.133Z","harness:codex","Verification evidence: complete sandboxed coverage rerun after the hosted regression fix passed 491/491 files and 7,280/7,280 tests. Exact counts are statements 52,820/52,820, branches 38,829/38,829, functions 10,854/10,854, lines 51,016/51,016; the exact coverage gate reports 100/100/100/100 with zero uncovered counts."
  "2026-08-13T23:53:28.679Z","harness:codex","PR #1005 exact implementation head 079303e2e passed every hosted deterministic gate, including aggregate exact coverage, Codecov patch, static quality, typecheck, compatibility, smokes, Windows, CodeQL, Semgrep, Trivy, TruffleHog, and benchmarks. Complete local verification passed 7,281 tests at exact 100/100/100/100 after review-tool hardening. This contract-budget tranche is delivered for review while broader mutation/stream every-command enforcement remains open."
  "2026-08-14T07:55:43.489Z","harness:codex","Completion evidence: every read uses generated format-aware budgets; complete-result list-all/--no-truncate intent now overrides default cost safely; explicit budget wins; row-compacted producer cursors rebase without skipping hidden rows; CLI/SDK/MCP expose unbounded recovery; token gate covers 79 surfaces with no regression. Packed npx and manual npm/Bun projects passed, and final coverage is 100/100/100/100 across 7,288 tests."
  "2026-08-14T08:26:30.528Z","harness:codex","Review disposition: reopened because live acceptance still covers every command and output format while current evidence proves the bounded read tranche. Reclassified pm-srns from blocked_by to implements: the delivered budget contract advances the uniform output grammar but does not require that broader item to close first."
notes[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T07:13:21.717Z","harness:claude-code","Output-cost baseline measured on the live 1,984-item workspace (2026-07-25) for the per-command ceiling work. Default TOON output, bytes and ~tokens at 4 bytes/token: pm get <epic> 20,290 B / ~5,072 tok; pm next 10,970 B / ~2,742 tok; pm context --limit 10 7,788 B / ~1,947 tok; pm list-open --limit 20 3,991 B / ~997 tok; pm search --limit 10 3,574 B / ~893 tok. JSON is consistently larger: context 10,860 B, next 14,857 B, list-open 4,777 B. The get depth ladder spans two orders of magnitude on one item — brief 2,241 B (~560 tok), standard 20,290 B (~5,072 tok), deep/full 153,058 B (~38,264 tok) — and standard is the default, so the common single-item read costs 9x the brief projection. Note deep and full are byte-identical by design (parseGetDepth maps full to deep; GET_DEPTH_VALUES is brief|standard|deep), so the ladder has three real rungs, not four. Static surfaces for comparison: root help 3,070 tok, full per-command help surface 49,872 tok across 76 commands, contracts --summary 900 tok, contracts --json 16,504 tok, contracts full 147,268 tok, MCP tools/list 9,523 tok across 31 tools."
  "2026-07-25T17:54:30.349Z","harness:claude-code","Output-cost baseline measured 2026-07-25 against the shipped build (2026.7.25, 2,022-item workspace). Bytes of stdout, TOON default versus --json versus --json --lean:\n\n| command | TOON | JSON | JSON+lean |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| context --limit 10 | 8,194 | 11,229 | 9,674 |\n| next | 11,632 | 15,464 | 13,871 |\n| list --limit 20 | 3,912 | 4,693 | 4,666 |\n| get <id> | 7,349 | 8,294 | 8,231 |\n| stats | 370 | 513 | 513 |\n\nJSON costs 13-37% more than TOON for identical information; --lean recovers roughly half of that on the two orientation commands and almost none on list/get. Both numbers matter for this item's degradation ladder: the ceiling has to be declared per command, and the encoding choice alone moves a command 13-37% before any content is dropped.\n\nDiscovery-surface costs, which have no ceiling at all today:\n- `pm --help` 12,281 bytes (77 commands)\n- `pm list --help` 6,173 bytes human, 21,941 bytes as `--help --json`\n- `pm contracts --summary` 3,598 bytes versus `pm contracts --json` 66,015 bytes\n\nThe 18x gap between contracts summary and full contracts is the shape this item wants everywhere: a declared default ceiling with an explicit opt-in to the full payload. It already exists on exactly one surface (pm-vxxm shipped it for contracts), and nowhere else.\n\nA two-call cold start (contracts --summary + next) therefore costs about 15,230 bytes today, roughly 3.8k tokens, before the agent reads a single item body. That is the number pm-ka6d's documented orientation path should be measured against, and the number this item's budgets should reduce."
  "2026-07-25T21:41:24.127Z","harness:claude-code","Per-depth output cost for a single item read, measured 2026-07-25 on the live tracker against dist 2026.7.25, using pm get pm-doxj:\n\n  --depth brief       2,241 bytes    (~560 tokens)\n  --depth standard   20,290 bytes    (~5,070 tokens)   <- the default\n  --depth deep      168,335 bytes    (~42,000 tokens)\n\nThe ratio is what matters for a budget contract: standard is 9x brief, and deep is 75x brief. The default sits at roughly 5k tokens for one item because standard includes the full body, and a roadmap or epic body grows without bound over the item's life — pm-doxj's body is a multi-year running document. There is no cap and no truncation notice at any depth, so a single pm get can consume a quarter of a small context window with no signal that it did.\n\nTwo consequences for the budgets this item defines. First, a per-command budget expressed as one number cannot describe pm get: its cost is a function of the item, not the command, so the contract needs a per-depth budget plus a body-truncation policy with a disclosed continuation. Second, --depth deep at 42k tokens is beyond any plausible agent budget and should probably carry an explicit warning or require acknowledgement rather than being one flag away from the default.\n\nRelated measurement for the same corpus question on pm-z71aoy: these figures come from the real 2,037-item tracker, not from a fixture workspace, and the spread between them is entirely item-data-driven — which is exactly the representativeness failure that item describes."
  "2026-07-28T22:35:30.625Z","harness:claude-code","Measured baseline and degradation-ladder design, 2026-07-28, on the real 2,176-item workspace at HEAD. Recorded here because the budget contract cannot be set without knowing what the current defaults actually cost, and because the shape of the waste is more informative than its size.\n\nMEASURED COST OF THE MANDATED AGENT LOOP. AGENTS.md instructs an agent to orient with four reads before doing any work.\n\n  pm context --limit 10                 7,075 B   about 1,770 tokens\n  pm next                               8,603 B   about 2,150 tokens\n  pm list-open --limit 20               4,713 B   about 1,180 tokens\n  pm search <keywords> --limit 10       3,980 B   about   995 tokens\n  total orientation                    24,371 B   about 6,095 tokens\n\nOther agent-facing reads on the same corpus:\n\n  pm get <id>                           7,813 B   about 1,953 tokens\n  pm get <id> --fields id,title,status    163 B   about    40 tokens\n  pm deps <id>                         63,725 B   about 15,931 tokens\n  pm validate                          22,754 B   about 5,688 tokens\n  pm contracts --summary                8,645 B   about 2,161 tokens\n  pm graph analyze                      1,617 B   about   404 tokens\n  pm aggregate --group-by status          457 B   about   114 tokens\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE WASTE. The single largest ratio in the table is not a slow command, it is a default. pm get costs 48 times its projected form for the same item, which means the expensive answer is what an agent gets by asking the obvious question and the cheap answer requires knowing a flag. Every ratio of this kind in the corpus has the same structure - a correct cheap form exists and is not the default - and that is the pattern pm-cxr0jb owns for projections, pm-mfy1ux for graph impact, pm-du93sr for health --check-only, and pm-okgxwa for explain-ranking. The budget contract this item declares is what makes those four stop recurring, because a declared ceiling forces a default to be inside it.\n\nFOUR MECHANISMS, in the order they should land.\n\nOne - intent-declared default projections. Each command declares, in contract data, the field set its declared intent consumes, and that set is the default. Widening is explicit. This is pm-cxr0jb and it is the highest-leverage single change in the table, because it converts the 48x get ratio and the per-row cost of every list surface at once. The declaration must be data so that the token gate can compute the expected cost of the default without running it.\n\nTwo - reference then dereference. Rows carry stable ids and bounded discriminators; prose bodies, acceptance criteria, and annotation text arrive only when a caller asks to expand. An agent scanning twenty candidates needs to distinguish them, not read them. Today a list row that includes a description pays for prose the agent discards in nineteen of twenty cases. This is the row-contract half of pm-sb0tns and must be decided with it, since it changes what a row IS.\n\nThree - per-response interning. Status, type, author, tag, and parent values repeat across every row of every response, and the id prefix repeats on every id. A response-level dictionary declaring the repeated values once and referring to them positionally is a pure encoding win that changes no semantics and no field set. pm-ez1dfg already measured the extreme case: per-row cursor overhead is 48 to 53 percent of the bytes in pm events. pm-kvxqp1 owns the general encoding study and this is its clearest candidate; it composes with mechanism one rather than competing, because interning shrinks whatever field set survives projection.\n\nFour - answer-shaped output. pm next answers one question - what should I do now - and costs 2,150 tokens to do it. The answer is one id, one reason, and the bounded set of alternatives the caller might override with. pm-cxr0jb covers the projection half; pm-08mt4k covers the correctness half, since the current recommendation can be a milestone gate with no children; and pm-okgxwa covers the explanation being sized by the workspace rather than by the answer. The three together are what makes next cheap, and none of them alone does.\n\nTHE DEGRADATION LADDER. A ceiling is only a contract if exceeding it has a declared, deterministic consequence. Proposed ladder, applied in order, each rung declared per command in contract data rather than chosen at runtime:\n\n  rung 0  full answer inside the ceiling, nothing omitted\n  rung 1  drop expansion fields, keep every row and every discriminator\n  rung 2  reduce row count against the declared ordering, and report the exact number withheld with a resumable cursor\n  rung 3  substitute a declared summary shape whose own cost is contracted\n  rung 4  refuse with a structured error naming the narrowing flag that would fit\n\nTwo properties make the ladder honest and both are currently absent. Every rung must state what it omitted, so an agent never mistakes a truncated answer for a complete one - which is the same requirement pm-q7qojt just closed for the list --all completeness claim. And the rung reached must be reported in the envelope, so the token gate can assert that ordinary corpora reach rung 0 and only pathological ones descend.\n\nWHY THE GATES CANNOT ENFORCE THIS YET. pm-9sui7t is in the current unreleased set precisely because both token gates were structurally unable to fail - one measured only help payloads, the other measured command output against a three-item fixture. pm-z71aoy owns corpus representativeness and pm-dpqa3h owns the orphaned measurement harness. Until a gate measures real output on a real corpus against the declared ceiling, every number in this note is a snapshot rather than an invariant, and the ceilings this item declares will drift the moment they are written.\n\nSCALE INVARIANCE. The ceilings must hold at both ends of the range the product claims. On a five-item workspace the whole corpus is cheaper than most ceilings and every rung must be a no-op, so the ladder must not impose a summary on an answer that already fits. At a million items the ceiling binds on every read and rung 2 becomes the common case, which makes the resumable cursor and the withheld count load-bearing rather than diagnostic. A single set of declared numbers cannot serve both; the ceiling should be declared per command and the ladder parameterised by corpus size, with the parameterisation itself in contract data so it is inspectable rather than emergent. pm-tch9cs is the fleet-level companion: per-command ceilings bound one answer and nothing yet bounds the aggregate the fleet pays."
  "2026-07-29T04:48:49.335Z","harness:claude-code","Baseline re-measured 2026-07-29 on the 2,186-item tracker, plus the degradation-receipt requirement the measurements force.\n\nCurrent cost table. stats 440 bytes, about 110 tokens. health --summary 570, about 142. get pm-doxj --depth brief 3,741, about 935. search with limit 10 is 4,205, about 1,051. list-open with limit 20 is 4,548, about 1,137. context with limit 10 is 7,694, about 1,923. next with limit 5 is 8,575, about 2,143. activity --compact with limit 50 is 10,568, about 2,642. contracts default 11,843, about 2,960. Root help 13,075, about 3,268. All 84 per-command helps together 212,496, about 53,124. activity --full with limit 50 is 671,002, about 167,750. contracts --full is 1,142,247, about 285,561.\n\nOrientation is cheaper than it was. context plus next plus list-open is now 20,817 bytes, about 5,203 tokens, against the roughly 6,095 tokens recorded earlier under this item. The contracts entry point in particular fell from 125,193 bytes to 11,843, a factor of 10.6, because the summary shape is now the default. That improvement is real and should be stated as such rather than re-litigated.\n\nThe spread is the ungoverned quantity. Cheapest useful command 110 tokens, most expensive shape 285,561 tokens. That is a factor of 2,596 across one product with no declared policy anywhere in between. Worse, the ratio between shapes of a single command is also ungoverned and is large: activity compact to full is 63.5x, health summary to plain is 233x on the earlier measurement, graph impact summary to default is 1,029x, contracts default to full is 96x. A budget contract that declares only a per-command ceiling does not constrain this ratio, and the ratio is precisely what decides whether an agent that picks the wrong shape survives.\n\nThe observation that makes the ladder design harder than it looks. In every one of those pairs the expensive shape is the only one that carries the answer. activity --full is the only projection that carries agent provenance. contracts --full is the only complete declared surface. health plain is the only shape with the extensions detail. The cheap shape is cheap because it omits the thing that was asked for. So a degradation ladder cannot be specified as cheap-by-default with an escape hatch; it has to be specified as a sequence of shapes each of which states what it dropped.\n\nDegradation receipts today, four commands and four different answers. pm list declares a projection object naming its mode and the exact fields it emitted. pm context declares sections_included and names them honestly, empty at brief, six names at standard, ten at deep. pm activity declares only the boolean compact, with no field list. pm get declares nothing at all: at brief depth its envelope is empty and it silently drops children, claim_state and linked, which are present at standard. pm search carries no projection key of any kind.\n\nAnd all four of the ones that do declare something name what is present, never what is absent. That is the wrong polarity for a budget contract. An agent deciding whether to spend more needs to know what it would gain, which is the complement, and it can only compute the complement if it already knows the full field set, which is what it was trying to avoid loading. The receipt must name the omission and the price of retrieving it.\n\nThe requirement this adds to the item. Every bounded read shape emits an omission receipt: which named field groups or sections were withheld, and the flag that would restore each. Row omission already works this way, with a count, a has_more and a resumable cursor, so the pattern exists in the product and only needs extending from rows to fields. A shape that withholds nothing says so explicitly rather than omitting the receipt, because an absent receipt and a complete payload must not look alike.\n\nA second requirement follows for the top of the ladder. A complete surface that no consumer can load is indistinguishable from an absent one. contracts --full at 285,561 tokens has never been read by any agent and never will be. The full form should be paged or queryable against the same predicate grammar as the rest of the product rather than emitted whole, and the ceiling contract should forbid any shape whose declared budget exceeds a working context window without a paging affordance."
